{
  "story": "How does k-means group points? Start with six fixed points and two chosen centers. Assign each point to its nearest center, then replace each center with its cluster mean. Watch memberships change while points stay fixed. Finally, test another assignment: unchanged memberships reveal stable clusters.",
  "aha": {
    "stateId": "stable",
    "observation": "Both centers equal their cluster means, and another nearest-center assignment changes nothing: the clusters are stable."
  },
  "layout": {
    "preset": "approved-card-shell",
    "width": 640
  },
  "parts": [
    {
      "id": "steps",
      "kind": "step-rail",
      "presentation": "Place below the explanation and above the experiment. Show ordered state labels, the active step, and completed-step trace. Steps represent algorithm states, never elapsed time.",
      "bind": {
        "active": "state.id",
        "items": "variant.states"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "points-and-centers",
      "kind": "fixed-picture",
      "presentation": "Use the canonical algorithm/collection composition: six fixed point cells above two fixed center cards. Cell positions indicate coordinate order, not distance. Each point cell stacks its name, coordinate, and cluster membership. Center cards identify their seed or mean contributors; center values appear only in readouts. Region phases align with variant.states; select the entry matching the active state. Highlight state.active regions without moving cells. Keep cluster names visible rather than relying on color. Above the experiment, declare one-dimensional coordinates, arbitrary distance units u, fixed k=2, and the displayed initial seeds. Assume nonempty clusters throughout this run. This example demonstrates local convergence, not guaranteed global optimality.",
      "bind": {
        "active": "state.active",
        "links": "variant.links",
        "regions": "variant.regions"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism",
      "kind": "active-callout",
      "presentation": "Anchor beside the named active region without covering cells. Show the current action and its reason. Callouts identify where and why; readouts contain numerical results.",
      "bind": {
        "reason": "state.why",
        "target": "state.calloutRegion",
        "text": "state.sentence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "measurements",
      "kind": "value-readout",
      "presentation": "Place governing rules above the experiment and bound results below it. Assignment chooses argminⱼ |xᵢ − cⱼ|²; exact ties choose Cluster A. Update cⱼ = Σ{xᵢ : zᵢ=j}/nⱼ, where nⱼ counts assigned points. SSE = Σᵢ(xᵢ − c[zᵢ])², measured in u². Compute results from the active memberships and exact center expressions. Keep exact fractions internally; display centers and SSE to two decimals. Changed memberships compares consecutive assignment passes; initial assignments are identified separately. During mean updates, memberships remain fixed; during assignments, centers remain fixed. Phase supplies the bound status line.",
      "bind": {
        "values": "state.readouts"
      }
    }
  ],
  "controls": [
    {
      "id": "step",
      "kind": "index",
      "presentation": "Detailed parameter-control budget: zero. Navigation budget: one index control, plus Reset. Default is the first state; bounds are first through last state; increment is one state. Use Previous and Next, retaining those names when disabled at boundaries. All states persist until learner action, including the aha state. Next changes memberships, centers, or the convergence test, not merely labels. Provide visible keyboard focus and Enter or Space activation. Reference captures show the stable state.",
      "items": "variant.states",
      "label": "Algorithm state"
    },
    {
      "id": "reset",
      "kind": "reset",
      "presentation": "Restore the first state, initial centers, and unassigned memberships. Accessible name is Reset. Provide visible keyboard focus and Enter or Space activation.",
      "label": "Reset"
    }
  ],
